--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0610 (PR #1274) set out to regenerate three artifacts assumed stale from
the 0591 producer change, and found the real cause was different: the committed
copies predated the corpus that `dvc.lock` now pins. The same drift was then
found by accident in `tab_network_limitations.csv` (ticket 0625), whose numbers
were quoted verbatim to RDJ26561 reviewers. Two live instances found without
looking means the class deserves one systematic pass.

## Relevant files

- `deliverables/_shared/tables/`, `deliverables/_shared/figures/`,
  `deliverables/_shared/_includes/` — git-tracked Phase-2 outputs
- `scripts/analysis/*.mk`, `paths.mk` — the producers and their wiring

## Actions

1. In a corpus-present worktree (`make data`), rebuild every git-tracked
   Phase-2 artifact from the current pinned corpus.
2. Diff each against its committed copy; classify clean / cosmetic /
   substantive.
3. For substantive diffs, check the prose that includes or quotes the artifact
   (manuscript, tech report, data paper, revision responses) before committing.
4. Commit the regenerated set with the classification in the message. Anything
   touching a submission record is reported to the author, not auto-committed
   (precedent: 0625).

## Test

No red step — regeneration audit; determinism of the producers is already
pinned by `tests/test_hash_seed_determinism.py` and `make determinism-check`.

## Verification

- [x] Every tracked Phase-2 artifact rebuilt and diffed
- [x] Each diff classified; substantive ones reconciled with citing prose
- [x] Submission-record artifacts left for author arbitration

## Exit criteria

All tracked Phase-2 artifacts match what their producers emit from the pinned
corpus, or carry an explicit author decision to stay as committed.

## Result (2026-07-28)

63 git-tracked artifacts under `deliverables/_shared/{tables,figures,_includes}/`.
Producers were discovered from the Make database (`make -n <target>` per file),
not from a hand list. Rebuild: `make -j4 NJOBS=6` on the corpus `dvc.lock` pins,
`PYTHONHASHSEED=0` / `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=0` (already exported by the Makefile).
No producer in the set touches the null-model chain. Every rebuilt target's
mtime was checked, so "clean" means "regenerated and byte-identical", not
"make skipped it".

| Class | n | Members |
|---|---|---|
| Clean | 20 | `fig_composition`, `fig_composition_wide`, `fig_global_map_direct`, `fig_traditions_pre2008_citers`; `tab_citation_coverage_periods`, `tab_citation_verification`, `tab_corpus_flow`, `tab_corpus_sources`, `tab_dedup_error_estimates`, `tab_filter_ablation`, `tab_lit_confirmations`, `tab_network_limitations`, `tab_reference_counts`, `tab_reranker_validation`, `tab_retrieval_protocol.csv`, `tab_retrieval_protocol.md`, `tab_sem6_assignments`, `tab_semantic_robustness`, `tab_variables.md`, `tab_venues.md` |
| Cosmetic | 0 | — |
| Substantive, left as committed | 5 | `fig_bars_v1.png`, `fig_breaks.png`, `fig_global_map_cocitation.png`, `tab_null_separation_pre2007.csv`, `tab_pre2007_coverage.csv` |
| No producer | 37 | 34 `_includes/*.md`, `figures/scratch/scout_tradition_coupling.png`, `tab_venues_fr.md`, `tab_venues_fr_caption.md` |
| Excluded | 1 | `qa_cocitation_edges_report.json` (author decision: dated live-Crossref probe of record) |

Today's earlier merges all verify clean: 0610 (`tab_venues.md`,
`fig_traditions_pre2008_citers.png`), 0625 (`tab_network_limitations.csv`),
0640 (venue captions).

Findings that outlive this ticket, all reported rather than fixed here:

1. `fig_bars_v1.png` is not actually frozen. `manuscript.qmd` A.2 declares
   `@fig-bars` frozen on the first-submission corpus, but `plot_fig1_bars.py
   --v1-only` filters the live `in_v1` column of `refined_works.csv`, so the
   figure is recomputed from a mutable corpus; four year-bars moved. The
   committed PNG is the only record of the submitted figure.
2. `config/community_registry.yml` keys hand-curated community labels and
   colours on Louvain community *ids*. Its own comment warns they are stable
   only for a fixed seed; they are also only stable for a fixed corpus. On the
   pinned corpus 7 of 9 registered co-citation communities lose their name and
   colour, and `plot_fig_global_map.auto_label` renders three of them as the
   literal string `nan nan` (the ticket 0550 NaN-formatting class).
3. `tab_venues_fr.md` / `tab_venues_fr_caption.md` have no producer: they are
   hand-maintained French translations whose data body must be kept in sync
   with the generated `tab_venues.md` by hand. They match at HEAD; nothing
   would catch it if they stopped matching.
4. `tab_citation_verification.csv` is derived from a live-Crossref probe
   (`qa_citations.py`, 300 links) — the same class the author froze for
   `qa_cocitation_edges_report.json`. It reproduced byte-identically this time,
   so no action was needed, but whether it should also be frozen is an author
   call.

Prose that moves, for author arbitration (not edited by this branch):
`manuscript.qmd` A.5 quotes `84 nodes, 369 edges`, within-tradition share
`0.89`, null `0.37`, `z ≈ 25`, and "only about three co-citation-core nodes
match". The pinned corpus now gives 109 / 466, 0.878, 0.353, z ≈ 28.1, and
seven anchor nodes with eight edges.

`tab_null_separation_pre2007.csv` was regenerated, committed, and then put back
as committed. `tests/test_separation_provenance.py::test_a5_prose_matches_committed_csv`
reads those six figures out of the CSV and asserts each appears in the A.5
bullet, by design: the guard's docstring says a regeneration that updates the
CSV *and* the prose together stays green, and only a silent drift between them
turns red. Committing the CSV alone was that silent drift. So the artifact is
mechanically prose-coupled, the pair must land in one author-arbitrated PR
(0671), and until then the CSV stays as committed under the exit criteria's
"or carry an explicit author decision" clause. Worth recording as the general
shape: an artifact's *guards* say whether it is load-bearing for prose more
reliably than grepping the prose does.

`fig_breaks.png` went the same way, found by the adherence pass rather than by
a guard. `plot_fig2_breaks.py` draws a significance threshold at z = 1.5 and
plots 2015 and 2021 as "not a break" markers. On the pinned corpus the
window-3 Jensen-Shannon z for 2015 is 1.528 — above that line. So the
regenerated figure shows the Paris marker on the significance threshold while
the script's own docstring, `manuscript.qmd` L313, and
`.claude/rules/writing.md` all read Paris as a diversification and not a
rupture. The two breaks the periodization rests on survive and strengthen
(2007 cosine z 1.86, 2013 JS z 1.66); it is the act II -> III boundary claim
that moves, which is an author reading.

`tab_pre2007_coverage.csv` was the last to move, on the PR review's red-team
finding. It is the only artifact in the set with no consumer of any kind — A.5
cites it qualitatively ("coverage of the early literature is sparse"), which the
larger numbers still support — so it looked safe to commit alone. It is not:
`separation.mk` declares `separation: $(SEP_COVERAGE) $(SEP_NULL)`, and
`compute_pre2007_coverage.py`'s docstring says its numbers "decide the
interpretive regime for the separation null model". Committing one half of that
pair while reverting the other would put A.5's evidence on two corpus snapshots
— and `docs/audit-manuscript-stat-provenance.md` handoff item 2 is *already* an
unresolved two-snapshot confusion about this same pre-2007 graph (84/369 vs
169/1,056). Manufacturing a second one to save a one-file diff is the wrong
trade, so the pair goes to 0671 together.

So the audit commits nothing. That is the honest result: of 63 artifacts, 20
are proven reproducible against the pinned corpus, 37 have no producer, 1 is
excluded, and all 5 that moved are blocked on an author reading. The
deliverable is the classification and the findings, and the exit criteria
anticipate this — "or carry an explicit author decision to stay as committed".

The generalisation, since the four coupled artifacts were each found by a
different means: **what makes an artifact prose-coupled is not always a guard
or a quoted number.** Here it was, in turn, a failing guard
(`tab_null_separation_pre2007.csv`), a threshold and marker labels hardcoded in
a plotting script (`fig_breaks.png`), an explicit freeze claim in the appendix
(`fig_bars_v1.png`), and a Makefile pairing plus a producer docstring
(`tab_pre2007_coverage.csv`). Grepping the citing prose for each artifact's
numbers found none of the four.

Follow-ups filed: 0670 (community registry keyed on unstable Louvain ids, and
the `nan nan` label), 0671 (manuscript A.2 freeze claim, the A.5 numbers, and
the `@fig-breaks` Paris marker).
